    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Why Are the Fishing Boats in Câmara de Lobos So Colourful?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>30 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Mostly tradition and each owner&#x27;s taste. Families paint their small wooden boats, the xavelhas, in strong stripes of red, yellow, blue and white, and no law tells them which. The only rule written on the hull is the registration mark on the bow, which EU law says must be in black and white.</p>

      <h2>What are the boats in Câmara de Lobos called?</h2><p>Xavelhas, roughly sha-VEH-lyah. The Priberam dictionary marks the word as Madeiran: a small traditional fishing boat used by the fishermen of Câmara de Lobos. It has a second, informal meaning too, a person from Câmara de Lobos, which tells you how closely the town and its boats are tied together. Visit Madeira calls the name Madeiran slang.</p><p>They are small, open wooden boats, the kind that work close to the coast, and they are what fills the little bay and the stony shore below the houses.</p><h2>So why are they painted in such bright colours?</h2><p>Here is the honest answer: nobody has written the reason down. We looked for an official or academic explanation and did not find one. What exists are several explanations that get repeated, some more solid than others.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Explanation you will hear</th><th>What we could find</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Each owner picks colours that are his own</td><td>The closest thing to a source. Visit Madeira's own writers describe the colours as reflecting each owner's personality, and the choice is clearly the family's.</td></tr><tr><td>Paint protects the wood</td><td>True of any wooden boat, which needs paint against salt and sun. It explains why the hulls are painted, not why so brightly.</td></tr><tr><td>Bright boats are easier to spot at sea or from the shore</td><td>Repeated widely, and it makes sense, but we found no primary source for it as the reason.</td></tr><tr><td>The law sets the colours</td><td>No. The rules cover the registration marks on the bow, not the hull colour.</td></tr><tr><td>Eyes are painted on the bow for luck, as in Malta</td><td>We found no source for this in Madeira. Do not expect to see it.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The simplest reading is the most likely one. In a small bay full of near-identical wooden boats, each family made its own unmistakable, and the habit stuck because the town liked it.</p><h2>Is anything on the hull actually required by law?</h2><p>Yes, one thing. Under EU fishing rules, Regulation 404/2011, every fishing boat must show its port letters and registration number on both sides of the bow, as high as possible, in contrasting black and white. The marks must never be covered or removed. Portugal's maritime authority applies the same rule. So when you look at a Câmara de Lobos bow, the black and white lettering is the state's part, and every colour around it is the owner's.</p><p>Portuguese fishing boats also traditionally carry a name, usually painted on the bow, and that too is chosen by the family.</p><h2>Do the colourful boats still fish?</h2><p>Some do, but they are not what brings in most of the town's catch. Câmara de Lobos is best known for espada. Espada, the black scabbardfish, made up about 43 per cent of the fish landed in Madeira in 2011 and about 53 per cent of its value. It lives deep, with adults most common between about 800 and 1,300 metres, and it is caught on long horizontal lines.</p><p>That work is done by bigger boats. In 2022 Diário de Notícias counted 23 boats in the espada fleet, with an average age of over 40 years, crewed by about 240 fishermen, roughly a third of all the fishermen in the region, on trips of about ten days. The painted xavelhas on the shore belong to the older, smaller-scale side of the same life.</p><h2>Does anyone still build xavelhas?</h2><p>Rarely. In 2020 the artist Rigo 23 and the master caulker known as Bailinha built a new xavelha on the Ilhéu, the fishermen's neighbourhood of Câmara de Lobos, which opened in 1949. Rigo 23 described it as the first boat built from scratch in the town this century. A calafate, the caulker, is the craftsman who seals the seams of a wooden hull, and it is one of the trades the boats depend on.</p><p>The boats that are no longer used do not always go quietly. The bay has a varadouro, a hard standing on land for hauled-out boats, run by the port authority, and in 2024 the port and the town council ordered abandoned boats to be cleared from it.</p><h2>Where do you get the best view of them?</h2><p>From the water, for the whole picture. Câmara de Lobos is the first town west of Funchal, and from a boat offshore you see the bay at once: the painted hulls on the stones and on their moorings, the houses stacked on the dark rock behind, and Cabo Girão rising just to the west. On the Chifbay <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> it is the first place you reach after leaving Funchal, and on the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a> you pass it twice, out in the daylight and back as the lights come on.</p><p>From the land, for the detail. If you want to walk between the boats, read the names on the bows and sit with a poncha on the square, go by bus or taxi and give it an afternoon. A passing boat shows you the bay for a few minutes. It does not let you stand on the slipway. For the streets, the square and where to eat, read the <a href="/posts/camara-de-lobos-madeira-guide">Câmara de Lobos town guide</a>, and <a href="/posts/is-there-a-boat-from-funchal-to-camara-de-lobos">whether there is a boat from Funchal to Câmara de Lobos</a> answers the obvious next question.</p><p>Winston Churchill chose the land view. In January 1950 he set up his easel above the harbour, at the spot now called the Miradouro do Espírito Santo e Calçada, where a plaque reads that he painted there in 1950. From a Chifbay deck you get his view in reverse: the bay looking back at the painter.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a xavelha?</h3><p>A small traditional wooden fishing boat of Câmara de Lobos, in Madeira. Informally, the word also means a person from Câmara de Lobos.</p><h3>Is there a law on the colours of fishing boats in Portugal?</h3><p>Not on hull colour. EU rules require the port letters and registration number on both sides of the bow in black and white. The rest is the owner's choice.</p><h3>What fish is Câmara de Lobos known for?</h3><p>Espada, the black scabbardfish, caught on long lines in very deep water. It is the most important fish landed in Madeira by weight and by value.</p><h3>Where did Churchill paint in Câmara de Lobos?</h3><p>Above the harbour, at the viewpoint now called the Miradouro do Espírito Santo e Calçada, in January 1950. A plaque marks the spot.</p>

      <blockquote>The state owns the black and white letters on the bow. Everything else on a xavelha belongs to the family.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

